pportunistic pathogen is one  where this particular fungus is found in  many humans and normal healthy human  tissues but sometimes if given the right  opportunity it can lead to an infection  called an opportunistic infection  examples of these types of fungi are  Candida albicans and Pneumocystis here  Evette see with Candida albicans it  causes a disease called candidiasis  worth brush  these are infections of the mouth or the  vagina that are result of Candida  albicans fungal infection Pneumocystis  your Apache is what causes you may have  heard of it it's called Pneumocystis  pneumonia  or sometimes you'll hear it referred to  as PCP now these are infections that  occur usually in immunocompromised  patients so in particular  Candida albicans cause infections mostly  in people that are immunosuppressed so  these would be patients with aliens or  newborn babies Pneumocystis pneumonia is  particularly a major danger to those  that have AIDS because they are  immunosuppressed so their bodies cannot  fight these potential pathogens to kind  of keep them in check  it's also possible particularly for  candidiasis infections for this to occur  and normal healthy individuals after  they've taken a regimen of  broad-spectrum antibiotics why is this  this is because as humans we have  bacteria all over our bodies inside our  bodies that are good symbiotic  mutualistic bacteria and one of the  things that they do is they kind of fill  this niche in our bodies where they use  available nutrients and they keep any  other things that are present from  overgrowing but if someone gets a  bacterial infection and then they have  to take a broad-spectrum antibiotic  treatment in order to cure their  bacterial right their bacterial  infection then of course the bacteria  that's infecting them will be treated  and will disappear but also so many of  these mutualistic bacteria meaning that  they can no longer prevent overgrowth a  possible fun fungi that are present so  this is one way in which can  albicans can actually cause an infection  and people that aren't immunosuppressed  now let's talk about another way that  fungal infections can occur in humans  this is through environmental reservoirs  so unlike opportunistic pathogens that  can actually be a part of the normal  human flora and then just cause  infections sort of when the opportunity  presents itself  these are fungal pathogens that can be  establishing an infection in human  following their contraction from an  environmental reservoir so first of all  let's talk about what diseases they  cause there's Histoplasma capsule item  which causes a disease called  histoplasmosis there is blastomyces  dermatitidis which causes a disease  called blastomycosis  and then there is Coccidioides immitis  it causes a disease called  coccidioidomycosis  since its kind of hard to say it has a  common name Valley now how do you  contract these these pathogens  Histoplasma capsule bottom is found in  animal droppings mainly bird droppings  and bat droppings blastomyces  dermatitidis is found in moist soil  Coccidioides immitis is found in dry  arid soils but all three of them are  endemic to the United States and as well  as other countries now how do you get  them from the environmental reservoirs  basically anytime that you are  disrupting the environment so if you are  disrupting the animal droppings or the  soil by gardening or digging or raking  leaves or camping or having a picnic you  run the risk of basically disrupting the  fungal cells releasing them into the  environment and in handling them that  means that the primary type of infection  for all of these these diseases is a  lung infection and the symptoms are  similar to that of flu or pneumonia so  coughing  beaver which can make them difficult to  diagnose if you don't know what you've  been exposed to also all three of these  while they are primarily lung infections  it is possible for them to disseminate  to other regions of the body this is  called becoming a systemic and infection  so they can spread to other organs and  even spread to the central nervous  system if this happens it results in  fungal meningitis which can be a serious  infection  finally let's talk about that third  grouping dermatophytes dermatophytes  caused infections of hair skin and nails  so while these infections are not as  serious some of the other ones we've  discussed today they it's more of a  nuisance so for example this would  include athlete's foot  entering worm  the result of these diseases are that  the infected area is usually red it can  become scaly and itchy but it can  usually be treated pretty easily and  it's not life-threatening so those are  our examples of human fungal pathogens  you can see that there's a lot of  diversity here but before I leave you I  do want to emphasize that there are  species of fungi that infect other  non-human hosts for example there are  fungi that infect plants so Dutch elm  disease and potato blight are caused by  fungi and these have important  agricultural consequences there are also  species of fungi that infect amphibians  so for example Petrecca katrien  denervated s is a fungal species that is  responsible for amphibian declines and  even amphibian distinctions around the  globe so certainly there are many  important fungal pathogens out there  that you should be aware of but that's  it for today thank you for watching and  I hope you learned a lot
 

